module.exports = /*#__PURE__*/JSON.parse('{"generatedAt":"2025-08-26T16:16:38.336Z","total":2,"tags":["memory","ml","post","project"],"items":[{"id":"L1VzZXJzL2tldmlud2FuZy9PYnNpZGlhbi9UaGUgUGF0aCBUb3dhcmRzIExvbmctQ29udGV4dCBSZWFzb25pbmcgMS5tZA==","slug":"camp-kesem","section":"misc","sections":[],"title":"Camp Kesem","date":"2025-08-26T16:16:38.020Z","tags":["memory"],"displayUrl":null,"image":"/Images/kesem.jpg?v=1756223976386","description":null,"links":[],"markdown":"---\\n  \\nI had the pleasure of being a counselor for the olden teen group for Camp Kesem this summer. It\'s \\n\\nPictured is me eating a taco (??) with mayo, ketchup, chocolate, and five minutes of salting for Chopped :\')","vaultPath":"The Path Towards Long-Context Reasoning 1.md","public":true,"readingTime":"1 min read"},{"id":"L1VzZXJzL2tldmlud2FuZy9PYnNpZGlhbi9UaGUgUGF0aCBUb3dhcmRzIExvbmctQ29udGV4dCBSZWFzb25pbmcubWQ=","slug":"the-path-towards-long-context-reasoning","section":"blog","sections":["ml"],"title":"The Path Towards Long-Context Reasoning","date":"2025-08-26T15:29:39.689Z","tags":["ml","post","project"],"displayUrl":null,"image":"/Images/long-context-post.png?v=1756216544025","description":null,"links":[],"markdown":"---\\n  \\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nModern-day Large Language Models (LLMs) are all variants of the transformer architecture, which alternates layers of sequence mixers (attention blocks) and feature mixers (MLPs). Attention blocks move information across tokens in a sequence by calculating a similarity score between a given token, the query token, and each previous token, the key tokens, and using this similarity score to scale the amount each key\'s learned value vector is added to the query token\'s residual stream, whereas feature mixers only transform tokens within its hidden dimension. Because of this, when the sequence length $n$ grows large, attention layers become a massive computational bottleneck. Long-context computation is essential for tasks like code reasoning and generation, and for problem-solving tasks where chain-of-thought models thrive.\\n\\nThis is one reason why LLMs have a maximum context length — typically ~250k for frontier models. The other is that many models will use Rotary Positional Embeddings (RoPE) to encode the position of a token, which applies a rotation to the complex plane for query and key vectors. However, because such rotations are inherently sinusoidal (i.e. they repeat with a period), so past a certain context length, the transformations applied by RoPE will begin to wrap around. Approaches like YaRN gradually extend RoPE to infinite-length context lengths during pre-training by rescaling the position indices before applying RoPE, but this still doesn\'t fully solve the time complexity issue.\\n\\nBecause language models are autoregressive, we can decrease the time complexity for next-token generation from $O(n^2)$ to $O(n)$ by storing our newly generated keys and values at every turn, so we only need to compute similarity scores for the token we are currently generating, instead of all previous tokens. However, we still run into issues: for large $n$, we won\'t have enough storage on VRAM to cache increasing numbers of high-dimensional key and value vectors. Moreover, to generate those $n$ tokens, our total time complexity would be $O(n^2)$, which is still too expensive for million-length contexts. So, KV cache compression isn\'t enough.\\n\\n### Linear Attention\\n\\nIn comes linear attention: instead of caching every individual key and value, what if we store a fixed-size matrix $M$ that the model has learned to compress all of the important past context? Concretely, if our keys are $k_1, \\\\dots, k_{n-1}$ and our values are $v_1, \\\\dots, v_{n-1},$ when generating token $n$, we can compute our query $q_n$ and then multiply by $M_n$ to derive the output $o_n = M_n q_n \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^d$. This gives us a simple calculation that is not more computationally intensive as $n$ increases. But, how do we update $M$?\\n\\nIn vanilla attention for iterative inference, we use \\n$$\\no_n = \\\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\n\\n\\\\frac{\\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left( \\\\frac{q_n \\\\cdot k_i}{\\\\sqrt{d}} \\\\right)}\\n\\n{\\\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} \\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left( \\\\frac{q_n \\\\cdot k_j}{\\\\sqrt{d}} \\\\right)}\\n\\nv_i\\n$$\\nto generate the outputs for the next token. If we want to turn this into the form of $M_n q_n$ for some updating matrix $M_n,$ we want some function $\\\\phi$ such that $\\\\kappa(q,k) \\\\triangleq \\\\exp(\\\\tfrac{qk}{\\\\sqrt{d}}) = \\\\phi(q)^T \\\\phi(k).$ Equipped with this factorization, we are now able to express the attention calculation as\\n$$\\no_n = \\\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\n\\n\\\\frac{\\\\phi(q_n)^T\\\\phi(k_i)}{\\\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} \\\\phi(q_n)^T\\\\phi(k_j)} v_i^T\\n\\n= \\\\frac{\\\\sum_{i=1} ^{n} \\\\phi(q_n)^T\\\\phi(k_i)v_i^T}{\\\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} \\\\phi(q_n)^T\\\\phi(k_j)} \\n= \\\\frac{\\\\phi(q_n)^T \\\\left(\\\\sum_{i=1} ^{n-1} \\\\phi(k_i)v_i^T\\\\right)}{\\\\phi(q_n)^T\\\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\\\\phi(k_j)}\\n$$\\nwhich means that now, we can use a \\"prefix sum\\" to make $O(1)$ updates to the state matrix $M$. A simple example of such a $\\\\phi$ is $\\\\max(0,Wx)$.\\n\\nHowever, errors will accumulate with only finite memory. For simplicity, we ignore $\\\\phi$, or alternatively, assume it is the identity; we also ignore the (normalizing) denominator. Then, $o_n = q_n^T \\\\sum _{i=1} ^{n-1} k_iv_i^T = M_nq_n$ where $M_n = \\\\sum _{i=1} ^{n-1} k_iv_i^T$. \\n\\nOne significant challenge in long-context tasks is for our model to perfectly, accurately retrieve a given piece of information. Concretely, \\n\\n# Improving the Regression\\n\\n\\n### MesaNet\\n\\n### Muon-Optimized Updates\\n\\n# Regression Alternatives\\n\\n### Forget Gate\\n### Polynomial Feature Maps\\n\\n### Replacing the Matrix\\n\\n#### Test-Time Training\\n\\n### Hybrid Architectures\\n\\n#### Gating\\n\\n#### Persistent Memory\\n\\n#### Caching Keys and Values\\n\\n# Unresolved Questions\\n\\n### Why The Regression?\\n\\n### Utilizing the CPU\\n\\n### Data-Architecture Co-design\\n\\nUnfortunately, high-quality long-context data is scarce. To fully make use of the our existing data, we can design hierarchical strategies to merge shorter chunks of data, and use curriculum learning (gradually increasing difficulty over time) as our pre-training schedule. (Note that curriculum learning is also how YaRN is used to increase context lengths).\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nI believe that efficient and performative long-context reasoning is one of the most important problems to solve on the path towards super-intelligence. If we trace back the last decade of AI, we have seen one consistent pattern — The Bitter Lesson, which posits that in the limit, models that effectively scale with increased computation will win. The genius of self-attention is that it lends itself remarkably to parallelization, and was designed with specialized GPU hardware in mind. This enabled us to see actualized performance gains with increased compute and larger model sizes, which were not replicated in RNNs and LSTMs at the time. The Chinchilla scaling laws hammered the point home: despite minor architectural differences, model performance really just boiled down to an equation involving dataset size and compute (which governs parameter count), and past a certain point, compute is much easier to reliably continue to source than high-quality data. The recent innovation in chain-of-thought models is simply an extension of this philosophy: it enables us to continue to scale compute for increased performance gains by using generated tokens as a \\"scratchpad\\", in a way that universal transformers of the past couldn\'t. My opinion is that this trend will continue, and optimized, efficient architectures for ever-longer chain-of-thought traces will be the next crucial innovation for tasks like building fully autonomous ML engineers or researchers.\\n\\nThis is also my first go at technical writing, so I\'d love to discuss any thoughts or feedback, and apologizes for the rough patches :) ","vaultPath":"The Path Towards Long-Context Reasoning.md","public":true,"readingTime":"5 min read"}]}');

